 "substitutedBy": null,
 "summary": "Relates to authorizing cull permits to certified nuisance wildlife specialists; authorizes the department of environmental conservation, after a review of the site-specific wildlife management plan, and upon a finding that wildlife has become a nuisance, destructive to public or private property or a threat to public health or welfare, to issue a certified nuisance wildlife specialist a cull permit; defines terms.",
 "title": "Relates to wildlife damage management, authorizing cull permits to certified nuisance wildlife specialists",
 "vetoMessages": {
  "items": [
   {
    "billId": {
     "basePrintNo": "A1599",
     "basePrintNoStr": "A1599-2019",
     "printNo": "A1599",
     "session": 2019,
     "version": ""
    },
    "billPage": 0,
    "chapter": 0,
    "lineEnd": 0,
    "lineStart": 0,
    "memoText": "\n \n                         VETO MESSAGE - No. 150\n \nTO THE ASSEMBLY:\n \nI am returning herewith, without my approval, the following bill:\n \nAssembly Bill Number 1599, entitled:\n \n    \"AN  ACT to amend the environmental conservation law, in relation to\n      wildlife damage management\"\n \n    NOT APPROVED\n \n  The  Department  of  Environmental  Conservation  (DEC)  is  currently\nauthorized  by  Environmental  Conservation Law section 11-0521 to grant\npermits that allow a person to take wildlife, including  deer,  whenever\nit  becomes  a nuisance, destructive to public or private property, or a\nthreat to public health or welfare. These permits may  be  issued  to  a\nfederal,  state or local government or a private landowner. For a permit\nto be issued, the entity seeking the permit must submit  an  application\nand  a  proposed management plan describing the necessity of the permit.\nThroughout the permit application process, DEC works with the  applicant\nto  establish  the  best strategy for wildlife management. If the permit\napplication is approved, DEC grants  the  entity  to  the  authority  to\nutilize  certain  techniques  for  a more effective cull. This authority\nincludes the use of nets, taking with the aid of lights, taking deer  in\nclosed  areas  of hunting, and additional measures that could not other-\nwise be used without a permit. In addition to the aforementioned permit,\nthe Department also issues Airstrike Hazard Permits to address  wildlife\nthat may impact air safety and Deer Management Permits under ECL section\n11-0913 to control overall deer abundance.\n \n  This bill, which would amend the Environmental Conservation Law to add\na  new  section  11-0522,  is defective in several respects, as it would\ncreate a new two-tier permitting process for wildlife management that is\nless flexible than the existing program, more  onerous  for  applicants,\nthe Department and municipalities, and would not provide any appreciable\nbenefit to the community.\n \n  This  bill would create a new permit that would serve the same purpose\nas DEC's existing permits, except only a  \"certified  nuisance  wildlife\nspecialist\"  (NWS),  acting pursuant to a wildlife management plan could\nbe issued such new permit. More specifically, the bill  limits  who  may\neven  be considered a NWS to an employee of a land grant university or a\ngovernment employee or contractor responsible for  wildlife  management.\nBy  narrowing the field of applicants to this permit program and exclud-\ning private landowners, businesses, and airport owners  from  qualifying\nas  a NWS, municipalities and airports will have fewer options available\nto address potential safety hazards and health risks.\n \n  Second, the bill is  problematic  because  it  would  require  DEC  to\npublish new regulations to establish criteria for an NWS before a permit\nmay  be  issued.  These  regulations would have to set forth the minimum\nlevel of marksmanship certification, required liability  insurance,  and\nreporting  requirements for an NWS. This would delay the ability to seek\nsuch permits.\n \n  Third, the bill would redefine \"wildlife,\" for the purposes of section\n11-0522, as wild game and all other animal life at an  airport,  however\nin  other  areas  of the state wildlife would mean only deer and coyote.\nThe clear intent of the bill is to allow the taking of wild game and all\nanimal life that may pose a risk to an aircraft at airports, but for all\nother areas in the state, the permit would only be allowed to cover deer\nand coyote. In this regard, the bill sponsor is operating on the assump-\ntion  that  outside  of  airports there are not myriad nuisance animals,\nbesides deer and coyote, that cause significant damage to  property  and\nthreaten  public  safety.  That  assumption  is flawed. In fact, DEC has\nissued a  significant  number  of  wildlife  nuisance  permits  to  take\nnuisance wildlife other than deer and coyote outside airport settings.\n \n  Fourth,  the bill is also structured to prevent DEC from qualifying an\nindividual as an NWS unless they are acting pursuant to a  site-specific\nmanagement plan. In effect, this means an individual cannot be certified\nfirst and then provide their services to a municipality but must instead\nbe  certified  for  projects  on a case-by-case basis. Given the limited\nclass of persons who could qualify as an  NWS,  and  the  fact  that  an\nemployee of a private airport could not be qualified as one, the bill is\nnot  an improvement over the current permit program and will not provide\na benefit to airports and other businesses who are impacted by  nuisance\nwildlife.\n \n  Finally,  the bill would only provide and NWS with a limited number of\nmanagement techniques such as the ability to bait within 300 feet of the\nroad, taking from a public road, and spotlighting a deer from a vehicle.\nDeer damage permits are not issued as a sporting license but are instead\nissued as a means to address a specific problem.  In  that  regard,  DEC\nshould  be  provided  with  the discretion to determine which management\ntechniques  normally  prohibited  in  a  sporting  situation  should  be\nutilized by a NWS to address a given nuisance condition.\n \n  In  all, this bill unnecessarily complicates the DEC's existing permit\nprocess while failing to provide a comprehensive  approach  to  nuisance\nwildlife management. As a result, I am vetoing this bill.\n \n  The bill is disapproved.                    (signed) ANDREW M. CUOMO\n \n                               __________\n",
    "signedDate": null,
    "signer": "ANDREW M. CUOMO",
    "vetoNumber": 150,
